    <h1 class="irm-h1">Medicare IRMAA Calculator 2026: Estimate Your Income Surcharge</h1>
    <p class="irm-date">Figures last verified against official sources in {lastReviewed}.</p>

    <!-- AEO Direct Answer Block -->
    <div class="irm-aeo-block" role="note" aria-label="Quick Answer">
      <p class="irm-aeo-label">Quick Answer</p>
      <p class="irm-aeo-text">
        In 2026, Medicare IRMAA surcharges begin when your 2-year prior MAGI exceeds <strong class="irm-gold">$106,000 for single filers or $212,000 for married joint filers</strong>. Tier 1 adds $81.20/month to Part B and $13.70/month to Part D. The highest earners above $500,000 (single) or $750,000 (joint) pay up to $443.90/month extra on Part B.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Problem Statement -->
    <p class="irm-problem">
      We noticed that many seniors receive an unexpected IRMAA surcharge notice from Social Security because their income two years ago was elevated by a job salary, property sale, or one-time retirement withdrawal. We built this free tool because seniors deserve a clear, instant plain English calculator to check their exact IRMAA tier without insurance broker calls, email signups, or selling personal data.
    </p>

    <!-- Who This Tool Is For -->
    <ul class="irm-who-list" aria-label="Who this tool is for">
      <li class="irm-who-item">
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="irm-who-arrow">→</span>
        Retirees with income above $100,000 who want to check if they owe an IRMAA surcharge on their 2026 Medicare premiums.
      </li>
      <li class="irm-who-item">
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="irm-who-arrow">→</span>
        Seniors planning large 401(k) withdrawals, Roth IRA conversions, or property sales who want to model the IRMAA impact beforehand.
      </li>
      <li class="irm-who-item">
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="irm-who-arrow">→</span>
        People who received an initial IRMAA determination letter and want to verify if they qualify for an appeal using SSA Form SSA-44.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

