       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="irm-section irm-section-tinted" aria-labelledby="scenarios-heading">
    <h2 id="scenarios-heading">Real-Life Examples — How IRMAA Calculations Work in Practice</h2>

    <!-- Scenario 1 -->
    <div class="irm-scenario-card">
      <div class="irm-scenario-header">
        <span class="irm-scenario-tag">Example 1</span>
        <strong class="irm-scenario-title">Henry, 68 — Single Filer with IRA Withdrawal</strong>
      </div>
      <p class="irm-scenario-intro">Henry is single and took an extra IRA distribution in 2024, resulting in a 2024 MAGI of $125,000.</p>
      <table class="irm-scenario-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Category</th>
            <th>Value</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>2024 MAGI (Single)</td>
            <td>$125,000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>IRMAA Tier</td>
            <td>Tier 1 ($106,001 – $133,000)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Part B Monthly Surcharge</td>
            <td>+$81.20</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Part D Monthly Surcharge</td>
            <td>+$13.70</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="irm-scenario-result">
        Henry pays <span class="irm-amber-highlight">$284.10 per month</span> for Part B ($202.90 base + $81.20 IRMAA) plus an extra $13.70/month on his Part D plan.
      </p>
      <p class="irm-scenario-action">Henry noted that because his 2025 income returned to normal ($85,000), his IRMAA surcharge will automatically drop back to $0 for the 2027 plan year.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Scenario 2 -->
    <div class="irm-scenario-card">
      <div class="irm-scenario-header">
        <span class="irm-scenario-tag">Example 2</span>
        <strong class="irm-scenario-title">David &amp; Susan, Both 67 — Married Filing Jointly After Retirement</strong>
      </div>
      <p class="irm-scenario-intro">David retired in December 2024. Their joint 2024 MAGI was $250,000 while working, but their current 2026 retirement income is $140,000.</p>
      <table class="irm-scenario-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Item</th>
            <th>Original 2026 Notice</th>
            <th>After SSA-44 Appeal</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>IRMAA Tier Based on 2024 Tax Return</td>
            <td>Tier 1 ($212k–$266k)</td>
            <td>Base Rate (Income &lt; $212k)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Combined Monthly Part B Cost for Both</td>
            <td>$568.20 / month</td>
            <td>$405.80 / month</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="irm-scenario-result">
        By appealing with Form SSA-44, David and Susan saved <span class="irm-amber-highlight">$162.40 per month ($1,948.80 per year)</span> in Medicare surcharges.
      </p>
      <p class="irm-scenario-action">They submitted Form SSA-44 with proof of David's retirement, successfully eliminating their IRMAA tier.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="irm-scenario-disclaimer">These are representative examples based on official 2026 CMS rates. Individual surcharges vary based on IRS tax records. Always verify your official determination with the <a href="https://www.ssa.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Social Security Administration</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ELEMENT 6 — COMMON PITFALLS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="irm-section" aria-labelledby="pitfalls-heading">
    <h2 id="pitfalls-heading">Common Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</h2>

    <h3>Assuming Municipal Bond Interest Is Exempt from Medicare IRMAA</h3>
    <p>Many seniors believe tax-exempt municipal bond interest does not count toward Medicare calculations. In reality, Social Security adds tax-exempt interest (Line 2a of Form 1040) directly back into your AGI to calculate your Medicare MAGI.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to include Line 2a tax-exempt interest when estimating your MAGI. Read official rules at <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/medicare/medicare-premiums.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Social Security Medicare premiums guide</a>.</p>

    <h3>Not Realizing Married Filing Separately Has Extremely Low Thresholds</h3>
    <p>Seniors who file Married Filing Separately face a harsh Tier 1 threshold starting at just $106,000 MAGI, pushing them rapidly into high IRMAA brackets.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to consult a tax professional to evaluate whether filing jointly provides lower overall healthcare and tax costs. Learn more at <a href="https://www.irs.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">irs.gov tax filing guides</a>.</p>

    <h3>Failing to Appeal When Your Income Drops Due to a Life-Changing Event</h3>
    <p>Social Security automatically applies your 2-year-old tax return. If you retired, divorced, or lost income since then, they will overcharge you unless you submit an appeal.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to file Form SSA-44 immediately. Use our <a href="/tools/should-i-appeal-my-irmaa/">IRMAA appeal wizard</a> to verify if your situation qualifies.</p>
  </section>

